From 2f0dbdde3f2fc62407ff7ddd71f27fe2d3caef4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?L=C6=B0u=20Oa=20Oa?=
 <212658678+luuoaoa@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 11:30:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Region-neutral build: locale comes from the OEM customization
 manifest

ApplyColorburstLocalAccountDefaults() pinned --cros-region=vn, which made
the OOBE language a property of the Chrome binary. Shipping a second
language would have meant a second full Chrome build of an otherwise
byte-identical OS -- hours of compile for one string.

Drop the region entirely rather than replacing it with a colorburst
mechanism. ChromeOS already has a file for exactly this:
/opt/oem/etc/startup_manifest.json on the OEM partition, read by
StartupCustomizationDocument, carrying initial_locale, initial_timezone
and keyboard_layout. release/make-variant.sh writes it per variant. The
OEM partition survives install, OTA and powerwash, so one build serves
every language and a device keeps the personality of the stick it came
from.

Setting ANY region here would silently defeat that, including a
well-meant "us" default: a region populates the initial_locale /
initial_timezone / keyboard_layout statistics from cros-regions.json, and
StartupCustomizationDocument::Init() applies statistics over the manifest
unconditionally -- its comment says "If manifest doesn't exist still
apply values from VPD", but the code does not check whether the manifest
exists. Statistics always win, so the manifest only works when nothing
sets a region.

Having no region is a configuration upstream expects, not an edge case.
InputMethodUtil::UpdateHardwareLayoutCache falls back to xkb:us::eng and
its comment names this case by name: "The hardware keyboard layout is not
stored if startup_manifest.json (OEM customization data) is not present".
The locale falls back to Chrome's own default, en-US.

Vietnamese is now a variant rather than the default. Nothing Vietnamese
is lost: the IME, the Telex/VNI/VIQR methods and the vi translations all
live in the same image and switch on with the locale.
---
 chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc b/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
index a8aa682ba2..be55c05b16 100644
--- a/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
+++ b/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
@@ -1119,13 +1119,29 @@ void ApplyColorburstLocalAccountDefaults() {
       command_line->AppendSwitch(flag);
     }
   }
-  // Vietnamese by default: cros-regions.json carries a "vn" entry, and
-  // --cros-region overrides the VPD region, which generic hardware does not
-  // have. Gives Vietnamese OOBE, Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh and the Vietnamese keyboard
-  // layouts.
-  if (!command_line->HasSwitch(ash::switches::kCrosRegion)) {
-    command_line->AppendSwitchASCII(ash::switches::kCrosRegion, "vn");
-  }
+  // No --cros-region here, and deliberately none anywhere else either. It used
+  // to be pinned to "vn", which made the language a property of the binary:
+  // shipping a second language meant a second 4-hour Chrome build of an
+  // otherwise identical OS.
+  //
+  // colorburst gets its locale, timezone and keyboard from the OEM
+  // customization manifest that ChromeOS already defines --
+  // /opt/oem/etc/startup_manifest.json, read by StartupCustomizationDocument --
+  // written onto the OEM partition per variant by release/make-variant.sh.
+  //
+  // Setting a region here would silently defeat that. A region populates the
+  // initial_locale / initial_timezone / keyboard_layout machine statistics from
+  // cros-regions.json, and StartupCustomizationDocument::Init() applies those
+  // statistics OVER the manifest unconditionally (customization_document.cc:
+  // "If manifest doesn't exist still apply values from VPD" -- but the code
+  // applies them whether or not the manifest exists). So any region at all,
+  // including a well-meant "us" default, wins over the file that is supposed to
+  // be authoritative.
+  //
+  // A device with neither is fine and is a configuration upstream expects: the
+  // hardware keyboard list falls back to xkb:us::eng
+  // (InputMethodUtil::UpdateHardwareLayoutCache, whose comment names this exact
+  // case), and the locale falls back to Chrome's own default, en-US.
 }
 
 }  // namespace
